### What is ECS?

ECS is AWS's own container orchestrator. You define a task definition -- the container image, CPU, memory, environment variables, and an IAM task role -- and a service that keeps a desired number of tasks running behind a load balancer. The concepts are few: clusters, services, tasks, and task definitions. Because AWS built it, it integrates cleanly with IAM, CloudWatch, ALB, VPC networking, and ECR, and there is no control-plane fee.

```java
// ECS task definition concept
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class EcsTaskDefinition {
    private final String family = "transfer-task";
    private final String image = "111122223333.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/transfer:1.4.0";
    private final int cpu = 512;       // 0.5 vCPU
    private final int memory = 1024;   // 1 GB
    private final String taskRoleArn = "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/transfer-task-role";

    public void explain() {
        System.out.println("Task: " + family);
        System.out.println("Image: " + image);
        System.out.println("CPU: " + cpu + " units, Memory: " + memory + " MB");
        System.out.println("Task Role: grants app permissions to S3, DynamoDB, etc.");
    }
}
```

### What is EKS?

EKS is managed Kubernetes. AWS runs and patches the Kubernetes control plane, and you run workloads as pods described by Kubernetes manifests. You get the full Kubernetes API, Helm charts, operators, and the cloud-native ecosystem. The advantage is portability: the same manifests run on EKS, another cloud's managed K8s, or on-premises. The cost is complexity -- you must understand deployments, services, ingress, namespaces, config maps, cluster upgrades, and add-ons.

```java
// Kubernetes deployment concept
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class K8sDeploymentConfig {
    private final String name = "transfer-service";
    private final String image = "transfer:1.4.0";
    private final int replicas = 3;
    private final String namespace = "payments";

    public void explain() {
        System.out.println("K8s Deployment: " + name);
        System.out.println("Image: " + image);
        System.out.println("Replicas: " + replicas);
        System.out.println("Namespace: " + namespace);
        System.out.println("Same manifest works on EKS, GKE, AKS, or on-prem.");
    }
}
```

### What is Fargate?

Fargate is the compute layer that removes servers from the picture for both ECS and EKS. Normally your containers run on EC2 instances that you provision, patch, scale, and secure. With Fargate you stop managing instances entirely: you declare the CPU and memory each task (ECS) or pod (EKS) needs, and AWS runs it on infrastructure it manages. This removes a whole category of operational work at a per-unit price premium over self-managed EC2.

```java
// ECS service with Fargate launch type
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class FargateServiceConfig {
    private final String serviceName = "transfer-service";
    private final String taskDefinition = "transfer-task:1.4.0";
    private final int desiredCount = 3;
    private final String launchType = "FARGATE";

    public void explain() {
        System.out.println("Service: " + serviceName);
        System.out.println("Launch Type: " + launchType);
        System.out.println("  - No EC2 instances to manage");
        System.out.println("  - No patching, no capacity planning");
        System.out.println("  - Pay per task for CPU and memory used");
    }
}
```

### How do you choose between ECS and EKS?

The choice is usually organizational, not technical. If a team is all-in on AWS and values simplicity, ECS is the pragmatic choice with less to learn and operate. If an organization needs multi-cloud portability, already has deep Kubernetes investment, or wants the K8s ecosystem (Helm, Istio, ArgoCD), EKS is worth its complexity.

| Feature | ECS | EKS |
|---|---|---|
| API | AWS-native | Kubernetes standard |
| Complexity | Low | High |
| Portability | AWS only | Multi-cloud, on-prem |
| Ecosystem | AWS services | K8s ecosystem (Helm, Istio) |
| Control plane cost | Free | ~$72/month |
| Learning curve | Small | Steep |

```java
// Decision helper
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class EcsVsEksDecision {
    public static String recommend(boolean multiCloud, boolean k8sExpertise, boolean simpleRequirements) {
        if (multiCloud || k8sExpertise)  return "EKS (portability, ecosystem)";
        if (simpleRequirements)          return "ECS (simple, deep AWS integration)";
        return "ECS (default for AWS-native teams)";
    }
}
```

### When should you use EC2 launch type instead of Fargate?

Use EC2 launch type when you need specific instance types (GPU instances for ML), when you need to run daemon processes on each host, when you have very high steady utilization where reserved instances make EC2 significantly cheaper than Fargate, or when you need access to the host OS for monitoring agents.

```java
// Fargate vs EC2 launch type decision
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class FargateVsEc2Decision {
    public static String recommend(boolean needsGpu, boolean highSteadyUtil, boolean needsHostAccess) {
        if (needsGpu)          return "EC2 (GPU instances not available on Fargate)";
        if (needsHostAccess)   return "EC2 (daemon processes, host-level agents)";
        if (highSteadyUtil)    return "EC2 (reserved instances cheaper at >70% util)";
        return "Fargate (default: no servers to manage)";
    }
}
```

### What is ECR?

ECR (Elastic Container Registry) is AWS's managed Docker image registry. You push images to ECR, and ECS or EKS pulls them using the task execution role or node role. ECR integrates with IAM for access control, supports image scanning for vulnerabilities, and handles lifecycle policies to clean up old images.

```java
// Pushing an image to ECR
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class EcrWorkflow {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. Build image: docker build -t transfer:1.4.0 .");
        System.out.println("2. Tag: docker tag transfer:1.4.0 111122223333.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/transfer:1.4.0");
        System.out.println("3. Login: aws ecr get-login-password | docker login ...");
        System.out.println("4. Push: docker push 111122223333.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/transfer:1.4.0");
        System.out.println("5. ECS/EKS pulls the image using execution role credentials.");
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is choosing EKS just because Kubernetes is popular, even when the team has no Kubernetes experience and the workload is a simple web application that ECS handles perfectly. The operational overhead of EKS, managing namespaces, RBAC policies, Helm charts, and cluster upgrades, is significant and unjustified for simple workloads.

Another mistake is running Fargate for workloads that need GPU access or specific instance types. Fargate does not support GPU instances, so machine learning inference containers must run on the EC2 launch type.

A third mistake is forgetting that Fargate tasks have ephemeral storage limits. If the banking batch job writes large temporary files during processing, the 20 GB default ephemeral storage may not be enough, and the task will fail silently.

```java
package com.bank.aws;

public class ContainerMistakes {
    // WRONG: choosing EKS for a simple CRUD app with no K8s experience
    // Team spends weeks on cluster setup instead of shipping features

    // CORRECT: use ECS + Fargate for simple web services
    // Reserve EKS for complex microservice meshes or multi-cloud portability

    // WRONG: running ML inference on Fargate (no GPU support)
    // CORRECT: use ECS with EC2 launch type and p3/g4 instances for GPU
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that Fargate is the default to reduce operational burden, and EC2 launch type is the exception for specific needs. They understand that ECS task roles and EKS service accounts (via IRSA) provide least-privilege IAM access per service. They also know that ECS services integrate natively with ALB target groups for rolling deployments, while EKS uses Kubernetes services and ingress controllers. For cost, Fargate Spot offers up to 70% savings for fault-tolerant workloads on ECS, similar to Spot Instances for EC2.
